{
  "text_input": "Patient is a 47-year-old female who presented with a 2-week history of cough with mucoid expectoration, high-grade fever with chills, shortness of breath (MMRC grade 2) with occasional wheezing, and throat pain. Physical examination revealed pallor and cyanosis (grade 2) with an initial SpO2 of 83% on room air. Systemic examination showed coarse crepitations and rhonchi in the lungs. Investigations confirmed atypical viral pneumonia, oropharyngeal candidiasis, and a new diagnosis of HIV (PLHIV status positive). The patient has a significant history of autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) with a previous hemoglobin of 4.6 g/dL, currently managed with steroids. Imaging showed grade 1 fatty liver, a renal cyst, and uterine fibroids. 2D Echo revealed mild pulmonary hypertension (PASP 50 mmHg), left ventricular diastolic dysfunction, and an ejection fraction of 57%. Treatment included antibiotics (Tazocet, Claribid, Bactrim), antivirals (Fluvir), antifungals (Flucan), steroids (Wysolone), and initiation of ART (TLD regimen). The patient improved and was discharged on oral medications with follow-up in Medicine, Hematology, and ART centers.",
